The three declarations, verified:

```js
if (true) {
  var v = 1;   // function-scoped — LEAKS outside this block
  let l = 2;   // block-scoped — stays here
  const c = 3; // block-scoped + must initialize
}
console.log(v);     // 1 (leaked — var ignores blocks)
// console.log(l); // ReferenceError — l is block-scoped

// Hoisting: var is usable (as undefined) before declaration
console.log(hoisted); // undefined (var hoisted, initialized to undefined)
var hoisted = 42;
// let/const hoist but stay in the Temporal Dead Zone until declaration
// console.log(tdz); // ReferenceError (TDZ)
let tdz = 'later';
```

The scoping difference (the interview core): var is function-scoped — the if-block's var v is visible OUTSIDE the block. let and const are block-scoped — they exist only within their nearest { }. This makes let/const predictable in loops and conditions; var was the classic source of unexpected-leak bugs.

The const subtlety (the most common follow-up): const means the BINDING is immutable, not the value. const arr = [1]; arr.push(2) is legal (the array mutates); arr = [3] is not (can't reassign). For true immutability: Object.freeze() or immutable patterns.

The decision rule: const by default (intent: won't reassign), let when reassignment is needed, var never (legacy code only).

```javascript
const user = { name: 'Ada' };
user.name = 'Grace'; // allowed
let count = 0;
count += 1;
```

